            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the new one: say the Latin for to delay, then the Latin for to stop for a while."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: inquilīnus, inquilīnī",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "inquilīnus, inquilīnī — \"a tenant, a lodger\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Inquilīnus dominō mercēdem dat. The tenant pays the landlord his rent."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Second declension, masculine: inquilīnus, inquilīnī m. Someone living in a house that is not his own; most people in a big city rented."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "inquilīnus is built on incolō 'to live in', from in- and colō 'to dwell, to till' (Lewis & Short, from an older incolīnus). EtymDB lists it without a parent."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
