          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "habitātor, habitātōris — \"an occupant, a dweller\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Domus vetus est, sed habitātōrēs eam amant. The house is old, but the people who live in it love it."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Third declension, masculine: habitātor, habitātōris m., from habitō 'to live'. English habitat comes from the same verb."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "habitātor is built on habitō 'to live, to dwell' with the suffix -tor 'one who' (Lewis & Short). habitō itself is a frequentative of habeō 'to have, to hold'."
            }
          ]
        },
